<h1><%= article.title %></h1>
<%- include('../partials/byline') %>
<p class="muted"><%= article.deck %></p>

<p>It is the smallest tattoo in this entire article series — a dot over a comma, smaller than a fingernail — and it may carry more meaning per millimeter than any design in the gallery. People who wear the semicolon rarely chose it for how it looks. They chose it for what it says about a story that did not end. If you are considering one, for yourself or in support of someone you love, this piece covers where the symbol came from, what it honestly means, the conversations worth having before you get it, and the unglamorous craft of making a very small tattoo stay legible for a lifetime — because a symbol this important deserves to still be readable in thirty years.</p>

<h2>Where it came from: a sentence that continues</h2>
<p>The semicolon tattoo grew out of the mental-health awareness movement that coalesced around Project Semicolon, founded in 2013 by Amy Bleuel, who lost her father to suicide. The metaphor she built the movement on is a writer's one: an author uses a semicolon when they could have ended a sentence but chose to continue it. The tattoo applies that punctuation to a life. It is worn by people who have survived depression, addiction, self-harm or suicidal thoughts — and by people who love them, lost them, or work beside them — as a mark that reads: my story isn't over. The symbol spread because it is quiet. It does not announce itself across a room or demand an explanation at a checkout. It is legible mainly to those who know, which makes it function the way the best symbol tattoos do — a signal to the initiated, a small private sentence on public skin.</p>
<p>A housekeeping note that matters to some wearers: the movement itself later reorganized and its history has chapters beyond the founding story, but the tattoo long ago outgrew any single organization. Whatever your view of institutions, the grammar of the symbol is settled and belongs to the people who wear it. Nobody owns a punctuation mark.</p>

<h2>What it means to the people who wear it</h2>
<p>Talk to semicolon wearers and the meanings cluster into a few honest shapes. For <strong>survivors</strong>, it is a milestone made permanent — the day the sentence continued, sometimes dated, sometimes counted in years when it is renewed or extended. For people <strong>still in the middle</strong>, it is a promise to themselves, placed where they will see it on the bad days: the inner wrist is the overwhelming classic, precisely because it is in the wearer's own eyeline, not the world's. For <strong>the bereaved</strong>, it is memorial shorthand — often paired with a name, a date, or the birth flower of the person lost. And for <strong>allies</strong> — friends, family, nurses, counselors, teachers — it is a small flag of safe-person status: I know what this means; you can talk to me. That range is worth understanding before you get one in solidarity, because strangers who recognize it may read your story in it. Wear it in support gladly — many do — and wear it knowing the symbol speaks first and asks questions later.</p>

<h2>Design choices: alone, or in company</h2>
<p>The plain semicolon, drawn clean, is the form's center of gravity and needs nothing added. But the design has grown variations, each shifting the meaning a degree. A semicolon whose dot becomes a <strong>heart</strong> tilts it toward love and the people who carried the wearer through. A semicolon worked into a <strong>butterfly</strong> — one wing trailing into the punctuation — is the transformation reading: the sentence continued and changed. <strong>Semicolon and arrow</strong> pairings point forward. Inside a <strong>feather, a wave, or a heartbeat line</strong>, the mark becomes part of a larger sentence about breath, survival or motion. Lettering combinations are common too: "breathe," "warrior," "still here," or a date in small script beneath. If you add words, read our <a href="/guides/stencil-day">stencil-day guide</a> on script minimums before you commit — tiny lettering is where good intentions go to blur, and a date rendered illegible helps no one's memory. The gallery's <a href="/gallery?cat=<%= encodeURIComponent(article.category) %>">Lettering &amp; Symbols shelf</a> holds small-mark designs built to stay crisp, which is the entire game at this scale.</p>

<h2>Placement: written where you'll read it</h2>
<p>Placement for this tattoo is usually decided by audience. The <strong>inner wrist</strong> is for the wearer — a sentence you read a hundred times a day, face-up when your hands rest. It is also the placement with the most history behind the symbol, and the one strangers recognize fastest. <strong>Behind the ear</strong> is the discreet version — visible in a ponytail, hidden by hair worn down, a whisper rather than a statement. The <strong>ankle</strong> and the <strong>side of a finger</strong> are the quietest addresses of all, though finger skin sheds and blurs faster than anywhere on the body, and a finger semicolon should be gotten with a touch-up already assumed. <strong>Over the heart</strong> and at the <strong>nape</strong> round out the common choices. Think, too, about the professional arithmetic that applies to any visible mark, and about sun: the wrist takes light all day, and at this size, contrast is survival — sunscreen is not vanity for a two-centimeter tattoo, it is maintenance.</p>

<h2>The craft problem: tiny tattoos age out loud</h2>
<p>Here is the part of the conversation a good artist will insist on. A semicolon is two small shapes separated by a gap, and tattoo ink does not stay exactly where it is put: over years, lines thicken slightly as the skin renews around them. In a large design, that softening is invisible. In a semicolon drawn too small, the dot and the comma grow toward each other until the punctuation reads as a blob — the one symbol that absolutely cannot afford to become illegible. The defenses are simple and non-negotiable. Size: most artists will steer you to roughly half an inch to an inch tall, however dainty your reference photo looks. Boldness: the mark should be drawn at a weight that looks almost too strong fresh, because it will not stay that strong. Spacing: the gap between dot and comma is the design; protect it. The <a href="/styles/fine-line">fine line style guide</a> explains the aging math in full, and our <a href="/guides/choosing-a-design">choosing-a-design guide</a> generalizes it: at small scales, simplicity is not a style preference, it is structural engineering. If an artist agrees to do your semicolon at any size, in any weight, without this conversation — that is the artist telling you something. Choose another.</p>

<h2>Getting one well</h2>
<p>The mechanics are mercifully small: a semicolon is minutes in the chair, often a shop-minimum charge, and an easy first tattoo — our <a href="/guides/first-tattoo">first-tattoo walkthrough</a> covers the whole appointment if it is yours. Healing follows the same rules as any tattoo, and at this size the temptation is to fuss with it; leave it alone and let it settle, per the <a href="/guides/aftercare">aftercare guide</a>. One last thought, offered plainly because this symbol deserves it: a tattoo can mark a turning point beautifully, but it is a marker, not a treatment. If the hard chapter is current, the strongest version of this tattoo is the one you get alongside real support — people, professionals, whoever your version of help is. The semicolon's whole meaning is that the sentence continues. Get it as punctuation for a story you are actively writing, and it will earn its place on your skin every single day you read it.</p>

